That is, we use digital money, when we carry out a purchase / sale transaction without physically exchanging coins or bills. Virtually all the money in the world is digital, with cash only accounting for about 8% of money in circulation.
By definition, virtual currencies are all digital, but not all digital currencies are virtual.
Crypto assets are the set of cryptocurrencies and other forms of goods and services that use cryptography, and by extension blockchain technology, to function. The best known crypto assets are cryptocurrencies or cryptocurrencies, but there are others such as smart contracts, tokens or governance systems.
Therefore, it can be stated that: all cryptocurrencies are virtual currency and digital money, but not vice versa, Digital money can be any currency in the world (Euro, Dollar, Yuan, Ruble, Yen), and that a virtual currency may not be a cryptocurrency, but a currency with a specific issuer. Definitely, every cryptocurrency is a token and / or a crypto asset, but not every token and / or crypto asset is a cryptocurrency.
Based on official information from the website of this cryptocurrency, Bitcoin is the first implementation of a concept known as "Crypto currency", which was first described in 1998 by Wei Dai, in a group (list) email group "Cypherpunks", where the idea of a new form of money that by using the crypto will be created and governed even day-to-day transactions, rather than being done by a centralized authority.
The result is that the BTC Currently it cannot be used as money due to ever-higher transaction fees and transfer times from hours to days, at many times. All of this is because the problems created by the Bitcoin Core blocks are exceeding certain reasonable limits for their own success as a mass and daily use cryptocurrency.
