Therefore, Segregated Witness (Segwit) or Segregated Witness in Spanish, in short, means separate (remove non-essential data within) a block's transaction signatures to make it bigger and more efficient.
Therefore, we can conclude that SegWit is an update to the transaction format that allows SegWit transactions to bypass the 1MB limit for block size, relying on the use of the new addresses under the bech32 format.
They are the units that make up a chain of blocks (Blockchain). Each block is made up of a hash (cryptographic code) that refers to the next block in the chain, in order to make it impossible for someone to remove or modify blocks, and therefore, to allow this to be detected by the members of the network.
The reason block creation is deliberately difficult is to prevent someone from spending bitcoins and then create and distribute their own blockchain where said transaction does not appear, which would give you the possibility to spend them again. When a valid block is created, it is distributed across the network, and work begins to create the next block.
SegWit offers a range of functionalities (advantages). Today the importance of its use has shifted from fixing the malleability of the transaction to solving the Bitcoin scale problem.
Not to be confused with SegWit2X, which was a proposal that proposed slight changes in the operations of the original SegWit but emphasizing increase the maximum block size limit to 2 MB by hard fork after six months (around May 23, 2017 by the time it was proposed). The Segwit2X after being implemented, over time the SegWit2x developers announced that the hard fork scheduled for November 16, 2017 was canceled due to lack of consensus among the members (miners) of the network.
BIP142 "Address Form for Segregated Witness." Discarded and replaced by: BIP 173
For Bitcoin Core version 0.16.0 already implemented since February of this year there are new features, various bug fixes and performance improvements, supported by current changes within SegWit, which translate into benefits for the end user, mainly by means of shorter transaction processing wait times.
The main objective is scalability, that is, the fact that the blocks have a 1MB hard-coded limit, which is not sufficient to efficiently manage the hundreds or thousands of transactions that users are trying to send every minute, and for which they have to wait in line until their transaction can be confirmed; for many hours and sometimes days, while the size of the network continues to grow, the intensity of the transaction increases, it is:
Although for these next few days they are already talking about a new fork within Bitcoin Cash (BCH) that will allow to carry from 8 MB to 32 MB the size of the Blocks.
