When we face the word cryptography or cryptocurrency we usually think of something dark, hidden or used for mysterious and unclear purposes. That idea is quite far from reality because although crypto means hidden, its objectives are fundamentally those of protect informationmaking it safe, unchangeable and unforgeable.
Any money issuing entity has as its most important mission make counterfeit currency as difficult as possible. It is for this and for nothing else that banknotes are so convoluted and have so many details.
What is clear is that money must be as unfalsifiable as possible. Otherwise, paper money could circulate in such a quantity that the system could not be sustained or it would cause a significant drop in its credibility and, therefore, in its value.
We could ask ourselves if everything we send could be read or listened to by other people and the answer is yes, it would not be complicated as long as the data travels as simple plain text data. That is where we begin to understand how interesting it would be to make unreadable what we are not interested in if it is read by someone other than the person who should receive it.
But when it comes to electronic money, the concern is the same as with physical money. In this case, there must be certainty that the money sent or transmitted is legitimate and also that it cannot be intercepted and used by people to whom that money does not belong.
The above binary text is also a weak way to protect information as anyone could see that it is a simple translation to binary numbers. So it does not offer more than a subtle protection, interesting for less serious things.
To get to something more interesting we could encrypt a text with a key. This would make that anyone who intercepted that text could not read it as they did not have the key that allows deciphering it.
La Enigma machine It is a mechanical symmetric encryption device that was used during the Second World War. Basically what it did was replace the letters of the text that was typed as in any typewriter by others that were determined by the key that had been configured by moving a series of rotors.
In general all cryptocurrencies use encryption systems that make them virtually unforgeable. When you send Bitcoin from one wallet to another, just like an encrypted email, two cases are verified
Bitcoin can only be used by the person who owns the private key of the address it was sent to.
Your wallet addresses (the one that is given so that they send you Bitcoin) are your public keys. Anyone can know them because they only serve to receive, not to use Bitcoin.
