Helping Skeptics Believe ( Part 1)
Why does the Catholic Church have such a narrow definition of marriage ?
The Church guards marriage , or holy matrimony very diligently because believes it to be a sacred thing : a divinely-ordained union between a man and a woman .
, openness to offspring ). For example , in Genesis we see that God made man male and female ; and that He called these two complementary sexes into union with one another in the great command to reproduce .
Jesus restored marriage to this ideal during His ministry .
To this the Pharisees responded , “Why then did Moses command one to give a certificate of divorce , and to put her away ?
Jesus was referring to a legal separation without the freedom to remarry—the separation of the spouses , but not the dissolution of the marriage .
The original Hebrew word here , though , is pornea , which is probably more accurately translated as “fornication , ” implying a sin that took place prior to the marriage , thus rendering the marriage null and void .
So , this would agree more with the concept of annulments , than with divorce .
In the Gospel of Mark , Jesus said , “Whoever divorces his wife and marries another , commits adultery with her ; and if she divorces her husband and marries another , she commits adultery” (10:11-12; see also Luke 16:18).
The earliest Christian historical writings beyond Scripture likewise defend the sanctity and indissolubility of marriage .
Let all things be done for the honor of God” ( Letter to Polycarp 5:2).
At about the same time , Athenagoras of Athens wrote , “We hold that a man should either remain as he is born or else marry only once .
“How shall we suffice , ” wrote Tertullian at the beginning of the third century , “for the telling of that happiness of that marriage which the Church arranges , which the sacrifice ( i.e. , the Eucharist ) strengthens , on which the blessing sets a seal , which the angels proclaim , and which has the Father’s approval ?
