What is baptism ?
To be baptized is to one is born again , to receive sanctifying grace , which God’s gift of spiritual life .
Note : Jesus was sinless because he was wholly-God and wholly-man .
Because he was wholly-God his thoughts and actions had to be – by definition one could say – the same as God’s .
So , why would he need to be baptized ?
Before ascending to heaven , Jesus reiterated his message to the Apostles , “Go therefore and make disciples of all nations , baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit , teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you” ( see Matthew 28:19-20).
In addition , in Acts 8:37, the Ethiopian eunuch , having received the Gospel from Saint Philip , expresses the desire for Baptism .
In his Letter to Titus , the Paul wrotes we are saved “by the bath of rebirth.”
In about the year 150, for instance , Saint Justin said those who were to be baptized “ are brought by us where there is water , and are reborn in the same manner in which we were ourselves reborn .
Around the year 200, Saint Clement of Alexandria wrote , “When we are baptized , we are enlightened .
Adopted as sons , we are made perfect .
In about 250, Saint Cyprian of Carthage revealed , “When the stain of my past life had been washed away by means of the water of rebirth , a light from above poured itself upon my chastened and now pure heart ; afterwards through the Spirit which is breathed from heaven , a second birth made of me a new man” ( Letter to Donatus 4).
In Genesis 1:2, it is written that at Creation , “The Spirit of God … moving over the face of the waters , ” and the ancient flood that purified the earth are both baptismal metaphors .
Baptism , which corresponds to this , now saves you , not as a removal of dirt from the body but as an appeal to God for a clear conscience , through the resurrection of Jesus Christ” ( see Peter’s first letter , 3:20-21; emphasis added ).
( He also speaks of the Old-Covenant rite of circumcision as the precursor of Baptism in his Letter to the Colossians (2:11-12).
