Perspectives Helping Skeptics Believe ( Part 1)
Sean receives inquiries from parishioners about bringing a loved one back to the Catholic faith , including cases when the individual no longer believes in God .
For those seeking advice on leading someone back to faith in God , it is crucial to understand and accept that faith is a gift .
It’s important to be patient and to listen to your loved one and to realize that if or when the time comes when he or she begins to reexamine their beliefs that god doesn’t exist , that person will usually need to pray for that gift .
It’s worth noting that believers often think nonbelievers are either just being difficult or expressing their anger at God for some past , negative event .
Because nonbelievers may have lost their faith for various reasons , each may need to have different questions addressed : there is no one-size-fits-all solution .
“ I am the true vine , and my Father is the vine grower .
You are already pruned because of the word that I spoke to you .
Just as a branch cannot bear fruit on its own unless it remains on the vine , so neither can you unless you remain in me .
Make sure that you are still actively attached to the “vine.” “ Remaining in Jesus” means more than attending Mass on Sundays .
Your prayers and actions will assure that life will continue to flow through the vine to ( and through ) you .
Prayers and expressions of love are far more important than having all the right theological answers—just as Jesus said that loving God and loving each other are the two most important commandments .
Consider anyone that you may know or may heard or read about who has abandoned the faith and returned . In almost every case .
Action do speak louder than words !
Think of it as “ praying ” the person into heaven .
St . Monica prayed for many , many years for son’s conversion .
( He was eventually named a doctor of the Church and was canonized. ) It’s possible that had God answered her prayers quickly—without that long struggle , during which Augustine reconciled ancient Greek philosophy with Christianity—there would be no St .
But piety is useful in all things , holding the promise of life , in the present and in the future .
Teach and exhort these things .
